update public.articles set content_en = content_en || $nh$<h2>🌸 Giving Drugs by the Vaginal Route (Vaginal)</h2>
<p>Drugs given by the vaginal route come either as suppositories or as a cream.</p>
<p>When inserting suppositories, the nurse should follow these points:</p>
<ol>
<li>Ask the patient to empty her bladder.</li>
<li>Wear sterile gloves to prevent contamination.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the drug is a cream, it is inserted with the applicator tube that most often comes with the drug.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vaginal-applicator-diagram.jpg" alt="Diagram of inserting a vaginal drug with an applicator tube"><figcaption>Inserting a vaginal drug with the applicator tube</figcaption></figure>
<h2>🌬️ Giving Drugs by Inhalation (Inhalation)</h2>
<p>Some drugs are given by inhalation to obtain a general or a local effect.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/inhaler-use.webp" alt="A man using an inhaler"><figcaption>Using an inhaler</figcaption></figure>
<h2>💧 Giving Drugs by Instillation (Drops)</h2>
<p>This means putting liquid drugs as drops into different body cavities such as the eye, the ear and the nose.</p>
<h3>Eye drops</h3>
<p>Eye drops are used to obtain a local effect, such as dilating or constricting the pupil for examination, treating congestion and infection, or relieving allergic conditions. A sterile dropper is used and hand hygiene must be observed when performing this procedure.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/eye-drops-adult.webp" alt="Instilling a drug into the eye of an adult"><figcaption>Eye drops in an adult</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/eye-drops-infant-illustration.webp" alt="Illustration of eye drops in an infant"><figcaption>Eye drops in an infant (illustration)</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Ear drops</h3>
<p>This means putting the drug as drops in the ear. It is used to obtain local effects, for example softening wax, relieving pain or giving local anesthesia, and killing organisms.</p>
<ul>
<li>For the patient's comfort, the drug or liquid should preferably be warm or close to normal body temperature.</li>
<li>Place the patient on his side with the ear to be treated on the upper side, then instill the required number of drops. The patient should stay in this position for a while to prevent the drug from running out of the ear when he moves.</li>
</ul>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>How to pull the pinna of the ear</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Adults</td><td>Pull the pinna backward and slightly upward</td></tr>
<tr><td>Infants and young children</td><td>Pull the pinna slightly backward and downward</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ear-drops-adult.webp" alt="Instilling a drug into the ear of an adult"><figcaption>Ear drops in an adult</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ear-drops-child-illustration.webp" alt="Illustration of ear drops in a child"><figcaption>Ear drops in a child (illustration)</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Nose drops</h3>
<p>The drug is put as drops into the nostril to relieve nasal congestion or to give local anesthesia, and a dropper is used to deliver the drug into the nose. The patient sits or lies down with the head raised and tilted backward. The patient must keep the head in this position for a few minutes to prevent the drug from running out of the nose.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/nose-drops-infant-illustration.webp" alt="Illustration of nose drops in an infant"><figcaption>Nose drops in an infant (illustration)</figcaption></figure>
<h2>🤲 Giving Drugs by Massage or Rubbing (Massage)</h2>
<p>Massage is the process of applying the drug as an ointment or a liniment to a certain area of the body and then rubbing it so that it is absorbed through the skin. The nurse must wear medical gloves. Massage is done by applying gentle pressure on the part to be massaged, with a circular motion for several minutes.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/topical-ointment-massage.webp" alt="Rubbing an ointment into the skin"><figcaption>Rubbing the ointment into the skin</figcaption></figure>
<h2>🍑 Giving Drugs by the Rectal Route (Drug Administration by Rectum)</h2>
<p>The main pharmaceutical forms that can be given by the rectal route are suppositories and enemas.</p>
<div class="nh-box nh-info"><p><strong>When is it used?</strong> Most drugs given by the rectal route are intended to treat intestinal inflammation or constipation, to relieve pain and to lower fever, especially in children. It is also used when other routes are not possible, for example giving sedatives during convulsions or epileptic seizures, or when there are wounds in the mouth or upper digestive tract or persistent vomiting.</p></div>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/suppositories-blister.jpg" alt="Suppositories in a blister strip"><figcaption>Suppositories</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/saline-enema.jpg" alt="A saline enema pack"><figcaption>A ready-to-use enema</figcaption></figure>
<h2>📅 Permitted Period of Use of Drugs After Opening</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type of drug</th><th>Permitted period after opening</th><th>Conditions and notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Oral liquid drugs that are already dissolved and ready (antipyretics, cough remedies, cold remedies)</td><td>3 months from the date of opening</td><td>With correct storage: close them well and keep them away from sunlight in a cool and dry place.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Drugs in powder form that need water to dissolve (such as antibiotics)</td><td>Set by the instructions written on the bottle, usually one week if kept outside the refrigerator and 10-14 days if kept inside the refrigerator</td><td>Follow the instructions written on the bottle.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Injections and liquid ampoules (Ampules)</td><td>Must be discarded after opening</td><td>Do not keep any of the drug left in them.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vials without preservatives</td><td>Must be discarded immediately after dissolving and using part of them</td><td>One common mistake is prescribing a 1g vial and splitting it into two doses, with the second dose given the next day. It is better to order half the dose, which is 500mg.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vials with preservatives</td><td>Up to 30 days from dissolving</td><td>With correct storage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insulins</td><td>30 days after opening</td><td>With correct storage, such as keeping it in the refrigerator or in a cool place so that it does not spoil.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Topical drugs: creams, ointments, lotions and paste</td><td>3 months from opening</td><td>With correct storage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eye drops and eye ointments that contain a preservative</td><td>Up to 30 days</td><td>With correct storage. If the drops or ointment do not contain a preservative, they must be discarded immediately.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Chest and nasal sprays</td><td>3 months from opening</td><td>With correct storage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Salbutamol liquid (Ventolin) used to treat asthma attacks in inhalation devices</td><td>One month from opening</td><td>With correct storage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nasal and ear drops</td><td>10-14 days from opening</td><td>With correct storage.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<div class="nh-box nh-warning"><p><strong>So you must record the date when you started using the drug, to know how long it has been open.</strong></p></div>
<h2>💉 Medical Injections (Injection)</h2>
<p>Although medical syringes are simple in structure, easy to use and cheap, they are of great benefit to the doctor and the patient. They are the best and fastest means of relieving patients' pain and giving first aid to the injured. An injection is a way of introducing fluids into the body by using a hollow needle and a syringe to penetrate the skin to a suitable length so that the substance reaches the body. In general there are four types of injection: intradermal, subcutaneous, intravenous and intramuscular, and in medicine it is a basic way of delivering drugs.</p>
<h3>A Brief History of Syringes</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>What the source says</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>The Pharaohs</td><td>Syringes have been known for thousands of years, since the Pharaohs began embalming their kings, after suctioning the soft tissues that decompose easily and then injecting molten "al-rabikh" (as the word is written in the source) into the empty skull through the nose. The syringe at that time was made of a hollow bone with a pointed tip, thin enough to enter the nasal cavity.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The Arabs</td><td>The Arabs knew syringes, developed them and used them in treatment. The first to describe them was the surgeon Abu al-Qasim al-Zahrawi in his famous book "Al-Tasrif li-man ajaza an al-ta'lif". The syringe he described was a cone-shaped tube with a pointed thin tip and a wide base, made of ivory, bone or metal, with a leather bag or an animal bladder attached to the other end. When it was pressed, the fluid was pushed forward. At that time the syringe was used for catheterization, to wash the bladder or introduce some substances into it, or to remove blood from the chest cavity or from wounds.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The 7th century AD (as stated in the source)</td><td>Iron syringes of various shapes also appeared.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The 16th century</td><td>Silver syringes were made, with the doses given to the patient marked on them, and strict conditions were set for their use, such as that nothing is injected except under the supervision of the doctor and that they are used only in certain diseases. But deaths after injection were many, because doctors at that time did not know about germs or sterilization methods.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1853</td><td>The history of medicine mentions that the physician "William Pravaz" was the first to make and design syringes in a form fit for use. They were developed later when the need appeared for a scale on the glass barrel to measure the amount of fluid.</td></tr>
<tr><td>After the discovery of germs</td><td>When the scientist "Pasteur" discovered germs, doctors realized that the deadly outcome that ended patients' lives was not caused by the disease itself but by the germs that attack the body. Since then, sterile syringes began to be used by boiling them in water up to 100 degrees. The need was also confirmed to make syringes from pure types of glass free of impurities, so that the drugs would not be affected by these impurities when they enter the bloodstream.</td></tr>
<tr><td>The modern era</td><td>The history of syringes was concluded by the New Zealand scientist "Colin Murdoch", inventor of the medical syringe designed for single use and then thrown in the waste bin, which prevented the transmission of infectious diseases through syringes.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<h3>Purpose of Giving Drugs by Injection</h3>
<ol>
<li>To obtain a general and rapid effect of the drug.</li>
<li>To prevent the drug from being destroyed by gastric juice if it were given by mouth.</li>
<li>To give drugs when giving them by mouth is difficult, as in unconscious patients or patients with nausea or vomiting.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Types of Injections</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Abbreviation</th><th>Site</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Intradermal</td><td>ID</td><td>Into the skin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Subcutaneous</td><td>SC</td><td>Under the skin</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intramuscular</td><td>IM</td><td>Into the muscle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Intravenous</td><td>IV</td><td>Into the vein</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/iv-route-vein-diagram.webp" alt="Diagram of an intravenous injection"><figcaption>Intravenous injection (IV): introducing the drug into a forearm vein</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/injection-angles-id-im-sc-diagram.png" alt="Injection angles for intradermal, intramuscular and subcutaneous injections"><figcaption>Skin layers and injection angles: intradermal (10°–15°), intramuscular (90°), subcutaneous (45° or 90°)</figcaption></figure>
<h3>On What Basis Is the Injection Route Chosen?</h3>
<p>Several points are taken into consideration when choosing the injection route, including:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>The amount of drug to be injected:</strong> if the amount is large, it is preferably given intravenously or divided between two sites.</li>
<li><strong>The type of drug to be injected:</strong> some drugs are injected only into the muscle or only into the vein.</li>
<li><strong>The speed of action of the drug:</strong> for example, we may need the drug to act quickly or slowly, which is decided by the doctor.</li>
</ul>
<h2>🧰 What You Should Know About the Syringe</h2>
<p>The syringe consists of 3 main parts: a needle, a cylindrical part and a plunger.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Needle</strong></td><td>Penetrates the tissue (muscle) to give the drug.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Barrel</strong></td><td>Holds the drug. This part is graduated like a ruler, either in cubic centimeters (cc) or in milliliters (ml).</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Plunger</strong></td><td>Used to draw the drug into the syringe or push it out.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Cap</strong></td><td>Covers the needle (shown in the illustration).</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/syringe-parts.png" alt="Parts of the syringe"><figcaption>Parts of the syringe: Plunger, Barrel, Needle, Cap</figcaption></figure>
<p>Syringes come in several sizes, for example: 1 ml, 2 ml, 3 ml, 5 ml, 10 ml, 20 ml, 50 ml and 60 ml.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/syringe-sizes.jpg" alt="Different syringe sizes"><figcaption>Different syringe sizes</figcaption></figure>
<h2>📋 What You Should Know About the Drug</h2>
<ol>
<li>The doctor's prescription (Prescription).</li>
<li>Be careful and make sure the patient has no allergy (especially with the first dose) AST.</li>
<li>Check the prescribed drug for color and clarity.</li>
<li>You must always know the name of the drug.</li>
<li>Choose the equipment suitable for the prescribed treatment and the injection site.</li>
<li>The indications (Indications).</li>
<li>The required dose, in ml and in milligrams.</li>
<li>The amount of drug is measured in cubic centimeters CC (Cubic Centimeters) or in milliliters ml (Milliliters). It is important to know that 1 ml equals 1 cc.</li>
<li>Look carefully at the bottle that contains the drug and make sure of the following:
<ul>
<li>The name of the drug.</li>
<li>The amount to be injected, and make sure the required amount is available.</li>
<li>The expiry date of the drug and of the syringe, which may be abbreviated as exp.</li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<div class="nh-box nh-info"><p>Drugs come in different types. They may be liquid or in powder form that needs to be dissolved, and they may come in pharmaceutical forms such as an ampoule or a vial.</p></div>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ampoule-water-for-injection.jpg" alt="An ampoule of water for injection"><figcaption>Ampoule</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-sterile-empty.webp" alt="An empty sterile vial"><figcaption>Vial</figcaption></figure>
<h2>🧴 How to Draw the Drug from an Ampoule</h2>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th><th>Details and reason</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Wash the hands</td><td>Wash the hands well with soap and water and dry them completely.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Inspect the ampoule and tap its upper part</td><td>An ampoule is a small, delicate glass bottle whose upper part is thin and narrow and empty. It may have a mark showing where to open it to make breaking easier. It may be colored or clear, and you may not see the drug inside it well. The upper part, which is supposed to be empty of drug when the ampoule is broken, may trap some of the drug. If the ampoule is broken in this case, the correct dose will not be obtained. So you must tap the upper part lightly with your finger until you are sure that all the drug is in the lower part and none of it is trapped at the top of the ampoule.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Break the ampoule</td><td>To break the ampoule, wrap the neck of the ampoule with an antiseptic swab and hold the neck with the fingers of your writing hand. Hold the bottom of the ampoule with your other hand and break the ampoule.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Draw the drug into the syringe</td><td>Put the ampoule on a flat surface, remove the cap of the syringe, insert the tip of the needle into the drug and pull the plunger of the syringe backward to draw the drug into the syringe.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Expel the air and adjust the dose</td><td>After drawing the drug, hold the syringe vertically with the needle tip upward and the plunger downward, and pull the plunger down until the upper part of the syringe is empty of drug. If you find air bubbles in the syringe, tap the side of the syringe lightly with the back of your finger until the bubbles rise to the top. Then push the plunger upward until the drug starts to come out straight from the needle opening and until you reach the correct mark for the dose you want.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ampoule-neck-mark.webp" alt="An ampoule with a colored mark on the neck"><figcaption>The ampoule and the mark showing where to open it on its neck (the arrow)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ampoule-neck-closeup.jpg" alt="Close-up of the ampoule neck"><figcaption>Close-up of the ampoule neck (the place of breaking)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ampoule-breaking-steps-drawing.png" alt="Illustration of the steps of breaking an ampoule"><figcaption>Steps of tapping the ampoule and then breaking its neck</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ampoule-draw-up-hand.webp" alt="Drawing the drug from an ampoule with a syringe"><figcaption>Drawing the drug from the ampoule with the syringe (1)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/ampoule-draw-up-syringe.webp" alt="Drawing the drug from an ampoule with a syringe"><figcaption>Drawing the drug from the ampoule with the syringe (2)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/syringe-vertical-air-bubble.webp" alt="Holding the syringe vertically to expel air"><figcaption>Holding the syringe vertically with the tip upward to expel air bubbles</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/needle-tip-drop.jpg" alt="A drop of drug at the needle tip"><figcaption>The drug coming out straight from the needle opening before reaching the required mark</figcaption></figure>
<h2>🧪 How to Draw the Drug from a Vial</h2>
<p>A vial is a small bottle with a cover made of either metal or plastic, and it is tightly sealed with a rubber stopper. It is important to note that what is opened and removed is the metal or plastic cover, while the rubber stopper is not removed. Instead, the tip of the needle is inserted through it and the drug is drawn from the bottle. It may contain enough drug for one dose or for several doses, and the drug inside may be a liquid or a powder that must be dissolved first before it is injected.</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-types-glass.jpg" alt="Clear and brown glass vials"><figcaption>Glass vials with a metal cover and a rubber stopper</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-powder-and-stoppers.webp" alt="A powder vial, a liquid vial and rubber stoppers"><figcaption>A powder vial and a liquid vial with their rubber stoppers</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-draw-up-1.jpg" alt="Inserting the syringe needle into the rubber stopper of the vial"><figcaption>Inserting the needle tip into the rubber stopper of the vial (1)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-draw-up-2.webp" alt="Drawing the drug from the vial with a syringe"><figcaption>Drawing the drug from the vial (2)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-draw-up-3.webp" alt="Drawing the drug from the vial with a syringe"><figcaption>Drawing the drug from the vial (3)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/vial-draw-up-4.webp" alt="Drawing the drug from an inverted vial"><figcaption>Drawing the drug from the vial (4)</figcaption></figure>
<h3>What to Do If the Drug Is in Powder Form</h3>
<ol>
<li>Remove the plastic or metal cover and do not remove the rubber stopper.</li>
<li>The doctor or pharmacist will guide you to the sterile liquid used to dissolve the powder. It is either distilled sterile water that you buy from the pharmacy or a solvent that is supplied with the drug.</li>
<li>Shake the bottle gently to speed up the dissolving of the drug.</li>
<li>Make sure all particles of the drug have dissolved in the solvent (leave it for a while).</li>
<li>Wipe the rubber top of the bottle with an antiseptic swab.</li>
<li>Draw the amount of the prescribed drug.</li>
<li>In this case you will need two syringes: one syringe to draw the solvent and add it to the drug powder, and another syringe to use for the injection. One benefit of this is that inserting the needle into the bottle to add the solvent makes the needle tip less sharp, which causes pain if the same needle is used to inject the patient, so we change the needle.</li>
<li>Check the name and dose of the drug before injecting.</li>
</ol>
<div class="nh-box nh-info"><p><strong>Drug information:</strong> It is preferable not to give or prescribe the following drugs in the evening or before sleep:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Vitamins:</strong> they stimulate the body cells and the nervous system, which reduces the chance of sleep and may cause insomnia.</li>
<li><strong>Diuretics:</strong> by their nature they will not let the patient enjoy quiet sleep.</li>
<li><strong>Headache analgesics that contain paracetamol with caffeine (Paracetamol + Caffeine medications):</strong> such as Panadol Extra, Adol Extra and others. Caffeine is well known as a strong stimulant of the central nervous system and it causes insomnia.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2>🔬 Intradermal Injection (ID) and the Sensitivity Test</h2>
<p>There are common uses of intradermal injection, including: testing for allergy to some drugs, the BCG tuberculosis vaccine for children, and testing for tuberculosis (Tuberculin Test).</p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-allergy-test.jpg" alt="Intradermal injection for an allergy test"><figcaption>Drug sensitivity test</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-bcg-infant.webp" alt="BCG tuberculosis vaccine for an infant"><figcaption>BCG tuberculosis vaccine for children</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-tuberculin-test.jpg" alt="Measuring the result of a tuberculin test with a ruler"><figcaption>Testing for tuberculosis: the Tuberculin Test</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Procedure</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Wash the hands well (Hand washing).</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Explain the steps of the work to the patient.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Put on sterile gloves.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Prepare the site to be injected (the surface of the forearm or the upper arm along the palm side) by wiping it with an antiseptic swab (Swab) in a circular motion starting from the center of the injection and leave the area until it dries.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Put a mark around the skin area where the test will be done.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Following aseptic principles, draw 0.05 ml of the substance to be tested (all air bubbles must be expelled from inside the needle to prevent a bulge under the skin, because it leads to an inaccurate reading).</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>Inject the needle into the skin area at an angle of 10 – 15 pointing upward while stretching the skin firmly and inserting the whole tip of the needle into the skin layers, noting the appearance of a wheal 2 mm in size during the injection.</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Dry the test area with a tissue, taking care not to press on the wheal.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>Tell the patient to wait 15 – 30 minutes without touching the test area before the reaction appears.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>Follow the patient to notice any signs of hypersensitivity (itching Itching, redness Redness, skin rash Skin rash).</td></tr>
<tr><td>11</td><td>After 15 minutes the extent of the resulting swelling and inflammation is measured along two perpendicular axes passing through the center of the reaction, then the result is recorded in millimeters.</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>Record the steps, the results of the test and any notes about the patient.</td></tr>
<tr><td>13</td><td>Monitor for any complications during or after the test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>14</td><td>Dispose of all the used equipment in its designated place (do not recap the needle, but put it directly in the sharps container).</td></tr>
<tr><td>15</td><td>Remove the gloves and wash the hands immediately.</td></tr>
<tr><td>16</td><td>It is preferable to give the first dose of the drug slowly to avoid any complications.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-site-forearm.webp" alt="Intradermal injection site on the forearm"><figcaption>Intradermal injection site: the surface of the forearm (the circle)</figcaption></figure>
<div class="nh-box nh-success"><p><strong>It is preferable to do the sensitivity test with a 1 ml syringe.</strong> An amount of the drug is drawn up to this graduation (the arrow in the figure).</p></div>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/tuberculin-syringe-1ml.png" alt="A graduated 1 ml syringe"><figcaption>1 ml syringe: an amount of the drug is drawn up to this graduation</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-needle-angle-diagram.png" alt="Diagram of the intradermal injection angle"><figcaption>The intradermal injection angle is 10°–15° with the blue 0.5 ml needle, used for local anesthesia and allergen testing</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-wheal.webp" alt="A skin wheal appearing during intradermal injection"><figcaption>The skin wheal appearing during the intradermal injection</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/intradermal-reaction-photos.webp" alt="Comparison of a normal test area and a reactive test area"><figcaption>Two photos of the test area (patient mark 10/02): the first with a mild reaction and the second with clearer redness</figcaption></figure>
<div class="nh-box nh-warning"><p><strong>If you have any doubt about the result of the test, ask for the doctor's opinion.</strong> When giving the patient the first dose of the drug after the sensitivity test, it is preferable to give it very slowly with good monitoring of the patient, because some patients may show a delayed allergy to the drug.</p></div>
<div class="nh-box nh-warning"><p><strong>Note:</strong> When the patient takes cortisone or other antiallergic drugs for any purpose, the signs of allergy may be delayed. So you must ask the patient whether he has taken any anti-inflammatory or antiallergic drugs, or whether he was given cortisone drugs in the operating room or the emergency room, for example. In that case the waiting time for the sensitivity test is longer than usual.</p></div>
<h2>🩹 Subcutaneous Injection (SC)</h2>
<p>There are common uses of subcutaneous injection, including: insulin injection (Insulin) for diabetic patients, and blood-thinning drugs (anticoagulants) such as Heparin and Clexan, and it is a route used to inject many drugs.</p>
<h3>Procedure</h3>
<ol>
<li>Confirm the patient's name (ask him his name).</li>
<li>Explain the steps of the work to the patient, stating the aim of the treatment.</li>
<li>Prepare the place to preserve the patient's privacy (Patient privacy).</li>
<li>Ask another nurse for help if the patient is a child, or an adult who is very tired or has disturbed consciousness or behavior.</li>
<li>Prepare the patient and choose the suitable injection site. If the dose is repeated, change the injection site.</li>
<li>Wash and dry the hands (Hand washing).</li>
<li>Put on sterile gloves (Gloves).</li>
<li>Wipe the injection site with an antiseptic swab (Swab) in a circular motion starting from the center of the injection, then leave the skin until it dries.</li>
<li>Remove the needle cap and hold the syringe in the hand that will be used for the injection.</li>
<li>Fix the thumb and index finger (the fourth finger) on the patient's skin while stretching the skin to raise the tissue under the skin.</li>
<li>Insert/push the needle quickly in one direction at an angle of 45 or 90 degrees (especially for heparin injection) while continuing to stretch the skin around the injection site.</li>
<li>Hold the syringe steady with the hand that is not in use.</li>
<li>Pull the plunger of the syringe back gently to make sure no blood vessel has been hit. If blood appears in the syringe, withdraw the needle and dispose of it, choose another injection site, and prepare a new dose, syringe and needle.</li>
<li>If no blood appears, inject the drug slowly to avoid tissue injury and pain.</li>
<li>Withdraw the needle from the injection site quickly and gently at the same angle of injection.</li>
<li>Observe the injection site for any bleeding or bruising caused by the injection.</li>
<li>Cover the injection site with the antiseptic swab and give a light massage to improve absorption of the drug. When giving heparin, do not massage, to prevent bleeding.</li>
<li>Dispose of the needle and syringe in their designated place.</li>
<li>Remove the gloves and then wash your hands well.</li>
<li>Record the prescribed treatment (time, date, treatment, dose, injection site, reaction to the drug).</li>
</ol>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/sc-insulin-pen-abdomen.jpg" alt="Subcutaneous insulin injection with a pen in the abdomen"><figcaption>Subcutaneous injection in the abdomen</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/sc-injection-skin-pinch.webp" alt="Pinching the skin and injecting the drug under the skin"><figcaption>Pinching the skin between the thumb and index finger during subcutaneous injection</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/sc-needle-angle-90-45.png" alt="Needle insertion angles of 90 and 45 degrees under the skin"><figcaption>The two subcutaneous injection angles: 90° with a 26 to 30 gauge needle of half an inch in length, and 45° with a 25 gauge needle of 5/8 inch in length, making sure the drug reaches the subcutaneous tissue</figcaption></figure>
<div class="nh-box nh-callout"><p><strong>Angle rule:</strong> when the needle (Needle) is small (1 ml syringe) the injection is given at an angle of 90, and when the needle is large the injection is given at an angle of 45.</p></div>
<h3>Subcutaneous Injection Sites</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Anatomical name</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Abdomen</td><td>Abdomen</td><td>From below the edge of the ribs to above the iliac bone</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upper outer side of the arms</td><td>Upper outer arms</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Thighs</td><td>Mid-thigh</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Upper outer quadrant of the buttocks</td><td>Upper quadrant of buttocks</td><td></td></tr>
<tr><td>Upper side of the back</td><td>Upper back</td><td></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/insulin-injection-sites.jpg" alt="Insulin injection areas of the body"><figcaption>Insulin injection areas: upper arms, abdomen, buttocks, thighs</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/sc-injection-sites-diagram.png" alt="Subcutaneous injection areas from the front and the back"><figcaption>Subcutaneous injection areas: front and back view</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/sc-90-degree-angle.jpg" alt="Subcutaneous injection at a 90 degree angle"><figcaption>Subcutaneous injection at a 90 degree angle</figcaption></figure>
<h2>💪 Intramuscular Injection (IM)</h2>
<h3>Procedure</h3>
<ol>
<li>Confirm the patient's name (ask him his name).</li>
<li>Explain the steps of the work to the patient, stating the aim of the treatment.</li>
<li>Prepare the place to preserve the patient's privacy.</li>
<li>Ask another nurse for help if the patient is a child, or an adult who is very tired or has disturbed consciousness or behavior.</li>
<li>Prepare the patient and choose the suitable injection site.</li>
<li>Wash and dry the hands.</li>
<li>Put on sterile gloves.</li>
<li>Wipe the injection site with the antiseptic swab in a circular motion starting from the center of the injection and moving outward to a distance of 5 cm, and leave the area until it dries.</li>
<li>Remove the needle cap and hold the syringe in the hand that will be used for the injection.</li>
<li>Fix the thumb and index finger (the fourth finger) on the patient's skin while stretching the skin gently at the injection site.</li>
<li>Insert/push the needle quickly and deeply in one direction at an angle of 90 degrees.</li>
<li>Hold the syringe steady with the hand that is not in use.</li>
<li>Pull the plunger of the syringe back gently to make sure no blood vessel has been hit. If blood appears in the syringe, take out the needle, choose another injection site, and prepare a new dose, syringe and needle.</li>
<li>Advise the patient to relax during the injection to avoid any tension or spasm in the muscles.</li>
<li>If no blood appears, inject the drug slowly and gradually into the muscle with light pressure to avoid tissue injury and pain.</li>
<li>Withdraw the needle from the injection site quickly and gently at the same angle of injection.</li>
<li>Cover the injection site with the antiseptic swab and give a light massage to relax the muscle and improve absorption of the drug.</li>
<li>Observe the site for any bleeding or bruising caused by the injection. If bleeding continues, press on the injection site. If signs of bruising appear, put ice packs on the injection site.</li>
<li>Dispose of the needle and syringe in their designated place.</li>
<li>Remove the gloves and wash the hands immediately.</li>
<li>Record the prescribed treatment (time, date, treatment, dose, injection site, reaction to the drug).</li>
</ol>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-injection-layers.png" alt="Illustration of intramuscular injection and skin layers"><figcaption>Intramuscular Injection: epidermis, dermis, subcutaneous tissue, muscle</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-vastus-lateralis-photo.webp" alt="Injection in the mid-thigh into the lateral muscle"><figcaption>Injection in the mid-thigh (Mid-thigh) into the vastus lateralis muscle (Vastus Lateralis)</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Intramuscular Injection Sites</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Anatomical name</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Upper outer side of the arm</td><td>Upper outer arms (Deltoid muscle)</td><td>The deltoid muscle</td></tr>
<tr><td>Middle of the thigh</td><td>Mid-thigh</td><td>The vastus lateralis muscle (about the width of a hand) in the area above the knee (middle of the thigh)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upper outer quadrant of the buttock</td><td>Upper quadrant of buttocks</td><td>The buttock is divided into four parts and the upper outer part is chosen</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-deltoid-area-nerves.png" alt="The deltoid muscle and nearby nerves and vessels"><figcaption>The deltoid area (Deltoid area) and the recommended injection site, with the nearby nerves and vessels</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-deltoid-landmarks.png" alt="Injection landmarks in the deltoid muscle"><figcaption>Injection landmarks in the deltoid muscle: Clavicle, Acromion process, Deltoid muscle, Scapula, Axilla, Humerus</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-thigh-90-degree.png" alt="Injection in the thigh at a 90 degree angle"><figcaption>Injection in the thigh at 90°</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-gluteal-landmarks.png" alt="Landmarks for locating the injection site in the buttock"><figcaption>Injection landmarks in the buttock: Iliac crest and Gluteal furrow</figcaption></figure>
<h3>Why Is the Upper Outer Part of the Gluteal Muscle (Upper Quadrant of Buttocks) the Ideal Site?</h3>
<p>This site appears very suitable because it:</p>
<ul>
<li>Is rich in capillaries, which speed up absorption of the active substance into the blood, so the patient's condition improves quickly.</li>
<li>Contains a small number of sensory nerves and pain receptors when the injection is given, so whoever receives the injection in this site (the upper outer part of the gluteal muscle) will feel relatively little pain.</li>
<li>Is large and deep compared with the other parts, so it accepts relatively large amounts of fluid. Therefore it is the more suitable site for injecting more than 2 ml and 3 ml, up to 5 ml. This advantage does not belong to the arm muscle, which cannot tolerate more than 3 ml of injection fluid.</li>
</ul>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-gluteal-safe-zone.png" alt="The suitable injection area in the buttock and the main nerve"><figcaption>The green part is the suitable site for the injection, and the main nerve (the sciatic nerve) lies away from it</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-gluteal-injection-photo.webp" alt="Injection in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock"><figcaption>Injection in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock</figcaption></figure>
<div class="nh-box nh-warning"><p><strong>Warning:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Do not inject into a muscle that is inflamed, swollen, hypersensitive or irritated, that has a mole or a scar from an old wound, or that is paralyzed.</li>
<li>When injecting an amount of drug more than 5 mm (as stated in the source), it is preferable to divide the amount to be injected into two parts and inject them in two different areas.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="nh-box nh-success"><p><strong>Points to remember:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Insert the needle for a distance of (2.5 to 5 cm) at an angle of 90 degrees.</li>
<li>The amount of drug to be injected is from 1 to 5 ml for adults and from 1 to 3 ml for children.</li>
<li>The muscle must be pulled upward before the injection.</li>
<li>Place the index and middle fingers on the upper front part of the muscle with light pressure to define the site.</li>
<li>Insert the needle into the muscle between the two fingers at an angle of 90 degrees.</li>
<li>Intramuscular injection for children under two years is best given in the mid-thigh, the vastus lateralis muscle (Mid-thigh), to reduce the risk of injuring large blood vessels and nerves.</li>
<li>Children older than two years can be injected in the upper outer quadrant.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="nh-box nh-error"><p><strong>Important:</strong> Intramuscular injection for children under one year is best given in the vastus lateralis muscle, to reduce the risk of injuring large blood vessels and nerves. Children older than one year can be injected in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock.</p></div>
<p><em>Note: the source gives the age in two different ways (two years on the points page and one year on the "Important" page), and both were copied as stated in the source.</em></p>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-infant-vastus-lateralis-photo.webp" alt="Locating the injection site in an infant's thigh"><figcaption>Injection site in the infant's vastus lateralis muscle: the junction of the middle third and the upper third (between the condyle and the greater trochanter)</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/im-infant-vastus-lateralis-diagram.jpg" alt="Diagram of the injection site in the lateral thigh muscle"><figcaption>Injection site (Injection site) in the vastus lateralis muscle (Vastus lateralis)</figcaption></figure>
<h2>🩸 Intravenous Injection (IV)</h2>
<p>Intravenous injection is one of the best ways of giving a drug, especially in emergencies, because the required result is reached quickly as the drug enters the blood directly. It is possible to inject into different veins of the body, and sometimes we have to inject into the neck veins, but the most common is injection into the veins of the arm and hand.</p>
<h3>Procedure</h3>
<ol>
<li>After observing the precautions mentioned earlier, do the following.</li>
<li>Ask the patient to expose his arm.</li>
<li>Tie a tourniquet on the arm above the vein you will inject.</li>
<li>Lower the patient's hand so that it points toward the floor and wait a few seconds until the vein becomes clear.</li>
<li>Touch the vein and make sure of it.</li>
<li>Disinfect the injection site using cotton and alcohol, and do not touch the vein with your hand again after disinfecting it.</li>
<li>Pull the skin below the vein downward to fix the vein.</li>
<li>Insert the tip of the needle in the direction of the vein with the needle tip raised (30–45) degrees from the skin surface, and the longer part of the needle parallel to the skin, that is, the needle opening faces upward.</li>
<li>To make sure the needle is in the vein, pull back with the syringe. If you see blood entering the syringe, you are in the vein. Release the tourniquet or ask your assistant to release it, hold the syringe steady with your left hand, and inject gradually at a suitable rate with your right hand.</li>
<li>After finishing the injection, put dry cotton on the needle site and then withdraw the needle gradually until it is completely out. Never massage the injection site.</li>
<li>Ask the patient to press on the cotton for a few minutes and raise his hand upward. He may bend his arm over the cotton. This is a very important step, because if he does not do it he will bleed.</li>
</ol>
<div class="nh-box nh-info"><p>Note: the numbering of the intravenous injection steps in the source skipped number 9. The steps are renumbered here in sequence without removing any step.</p></div>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/iv-tourniquet-diagram.png" alt="Illustration of the tourniquet and intravenous injection"><figcaption>The tourniquet (Tourniquet) above the intravenous injection site</figcaption></figure>
<figure class="nh-fig"><img src="/skills-img/drug-administration-routes-injection-guide/iv-injection-photo.webp" alt="Intravenous injection in the forearm with a tourniquet"><figcaption>Intravenous injection in the forearm with the tourniquet</figcaption></figure>
<div class="nh-box nh-callout"><p><strong>Remember:</strong> not all diseases are treated by taking drugs. Some patients may need a kind word, and a word may bring a soul back to life, and comforting the heart may heal the sick.</p></div>
<h2>📌 Quick Review: Key Points</h2>
<div class="nh-box nh-callout"><ul>
<li>Always follow the <strong>seven rights</strong>: patient, drug, route, dose, time, frequency, recording.</li>
<li>The fastest routes are the vein (30 – 60 seconds), then inhalation (2 – 3 minutes), then under the tongue (3 – 5 minutes), and the slowest is swallowing (30 – 90 minutes).</li>
<li>Check the name of the drug, its dose, its expiry date, its color and clarity, and make sure the patient has no allergy before injection.</li>
<li>Intradermal: angle 10°–15° and volume 0.05 ml, then wait 15 – 30 minutes to read the result.</li>
<li>Subcutaneous: angle 45° or 90°, and do not massage after heparin.</li>
<li>Intramuscular: angle 90° at a depth of 2.5 to 5 cm, and the buttock (upper outer quadrant) is the most suitable site for larger volumes.</li>
<li>Intravenous: tourniquet above the injection site, needle tip upward at an angle of 30°–45°, make sure blood returns before injecting, and do not massage the site after withdrawing the needle.</li>
<li>Record the date any drug was opened, and respect the permitted period of use after opening.</li>
<li>Dispose of needles in the sharps container without recapping them.</li>
</ul></div>$nh$ where id = 'a-skill-drugadmin' and length(content_en) = 14569;
